A||||20||Fiero5||When should a Fiero be parted out, and when should it be kept whole?||||1||
Z||000000||Fiero5||10-21-2000||11:23 PM||||I wanted to get some opinions of how each of you thought about this subject.<BR>When should a Fiero be considered OK to be parted out?<BR>On the one side: Should it be OK at anytime, even if (shudder) it runs OK, or has minor problems? The argument here being that it is your car to do with any way you see fit?<BR>What about a car that is in good shape, or has minor problems, but the owner decides to make some part it out due to the sad fact that it is possibly worth more in parts than as a whole car?<BR>How about if it's got some quirky problems, or the clutch is gone, and you don't want to put anymore money into it? Or it's constantly breaking down and you decide to sell the parts and recoupe some of the money you spent on it.<BR>Other side of the coin: Should it be OK only if it has major damage, hit hard, badly bent frame or rusted/rotted out metal, etc.?<BR>I realize that some Fiero parts are hard to find, and parting out a car is the natural course for aging cars. <BR>However, where do you "try" to draw the line or should you? Ultimately it is the owners right to do with it as he wants.<BR>However-have you ever seen a Fiero being parted out that was in better shape than your ride?<BR>Should we try to save restorable Fieros whenever possible, and say that only seriously "damaged" Fieros be considered used for parting out for the renewing of others?<BR>Let's hear your thoughts.||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||10-22-2000||12:03 AM||||Here at the Fiero Shop we will part out a car only when there is enough structural damage to make it un-restorable. Until then we try to restore and repair. If a car has been declared "totalled" due to damage of the structure or the frame then we will generally part it out. Even then, at times a frame can be straightened if it is not damaged badly.<P>We do keep the the space frames for sale to kit car builders too.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                          <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||10-22-2000||12:06 AM||||Today a friend asked me about doing a fastback conversion on an 86 SE.. I referred him to the excellent article on <A HREF="http://www.fiero.net" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.net</A>  and I decided to read through it again myself. The donor GT in the article looked to be in good condition, it just needed a new engine & exhaust system, and a good cleaning. However, it was parted out to upgrade an 86 SE (fastback clip, subwoofer, and I think leather seats)<P>Basically, here are my thoughts. if it has a good body (including little or no frame rust..) but a blown engine, it should be fixed. If it has a bad engine, transmission, suspension, etc.. then it's probably not worth saving. If a fiero has good mechanicals, but a trashed body.. it's probably worth it to restore. (or better yet, make a kit!)<BR>but if it's got a bad engine *and* a bad body/interior.. part it out.<P>Of course there are many other situations where the "rules" mentioned above wouldn't apply.. It'll be interesting to see some other opinions on this topic.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||10-22-2000||12:20 AM||||too many variables. if the only way to get your's running is to dissasemble another, you do what you have to. or you get another that turns out to be better than what you have, so you take parts off your first one. <P>or you find a car that has the parts you need that is cheaper than buying the parts separately or new.<P>or the fact that a car thats too far gone for one person some one with more skill sees as repairable.<P>off and on i post about my fiero that i got for $125. cheap for a parts car but even better 'cause for a few hundred bucks it's running again. it just happened that the repairs were within my abilities (and believe me they're limited). if i put it up for sale for what i have in it, some one might still decide it's cheap enough to be a parts car if the parts are more important to them.<P>ya gotta do what ya gotta do.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T<P>'87 coupe (feral)<BR>'84 indy<BR>'84 coupe (cheap thrill)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Shiner||10-22-2000||01:48 AM||||I am in the process of parting out my recently aquired '86 SE 2m6.  It has a locked up engine, most likely a worn out clutch, plenty of frame rust, loose steering column, and the title is for all practical purposes unrecoverable.  Personally, I feel it keeps the soul of the car (if there is such a thing) alive in a way, just like the way human reproduction does.  I think it is also a noble and worthy end to a great car.  Not as good as a triple digit fiery crash while racing, but almost.  <P> With that said, will everyone please share a moment of silence for this '86 SE that gave it's body and interior so my '87 GT could live on in glory.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||10-22-2000||06:49 AM||||:::silence:::||||reg||1
Z||000006||007DOUG||10-22-2000||09:22 AM||||Strip......<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87gtcrash.jpg"> <P>Rebuild on accident free frame......<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/indy4rf.jpg"> <P>Strip..........<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierorust.jpg"> <P>Save, It only needed a clutch and minor cosmetics......<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/bluegtupdate1.jpg"> <P>Here in the snowbelt states fiero rust, and some rust BAD. Why dump a ton of money into a frame that'll fall out from under the car in 5 years. Go south and buy one.<P>In the case of an accident, I only save "fender bender" fieros. Anything requiring replacement of the front core or a frame rack, isn't worth saving.<P>It all depends on how far the owner wants to go. If you have the ambition go for it. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||10-22-2000||12:07 PM||||I'd say it would depend heavily on the condition of the frame.  A frame that is damaged or heavily rusted is probably not worht the time and effort to restore.  Just two years ago, we put a new engine in my dads 87 coupe instead of finding a new Fiero.  It turned out to be the right move, since we have already gotten another 30K miles on her.  There is a lot of minor body damage to the car, but as a whole its nothing that cannot be easily repaired.  I've already re-built a cracked apart right front fender and repainted.  It came out good and only cost me about $10 for the fiberglass repair kit.  We probably won't do many major repairs in the future however.  The frame is very rusty and the floorboards are starting to develop holes.  The car is simply rusting out from underneath.  So the next major problem will probably turn it into a donor car.<P>Any other problems... body, drivetrain etc etc can all be repaired and restored.  It may also depend on the year.  An 88 GT is much more valuable keeping intact than say an 84 coupe.  (nothing against the 84's of course).  <P>Should we restore Fiero's whenever possible??  I think every effort should be made to keep them on the road.  That way the cars won't disappear as quick.  Although this may be a trivial point... the more that are still in use will continue to keep the parts demand high enough for manufactureres to continue to produce stuff.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/formula2m6/FIEROSIG1.JPG">  <BR>1988 Fiero Formula||||reg||1
Z||000008||2xsess||10-22-2000||02:06 PM||||I bought an 88GT from Kickhill that was seriously damaged on the passenger side frame and Bob gave me a frame section to repair it with, I paid $3500 for the car and almost all tha pieces to restore, it that situation one car passed to fix another. <P>The GT that Steve bought to convert his SE was a definite goner  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Aftertires_1.jpg"> <P>Major frame damage.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||10
Z||000009||TheFiero64||10-22-2000||02:37 PM||||I just parted an 86 SE that was the donor car for my 88 V6. It had body damage, Suspension trouble, tons of rust, a 4 speed with 3rd gear blown out, lots of missing parts, & a screwy title. I guess it was rebuilt prior fire. The whole interior Had this gross coating on it. It was so bad you couldn't tell what the gauges said. it looked brown inside instead of gray. The decklid was hacked because someone broke the lock. I still feel bad about parting it out. I will never part out another Fiero. I still have the frame. I was going to get rid of it yesterday but I feel like getting that por-15 stuff and a welder and fixing all the rust. I just can't have so many cars in my yard. I believe every fiero should be given a good effort to be saved. I am still trying to save for a 2.8 for my 86GT. I do have to get rid of some cars like my 82 Postal jeep & 85 Renault encore that has been in the family since the showroom. I don't need the neighbors thinking I'm running a salvage yard. I wish I had the funds to remake this parts car but I don't. If anyone wants the frame with its broken body parts you can have it for free.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Black88GT||10-22-2000||03:27 PM||||This is about Bob Schlag's gold GT he is parting out.  For those of you who do not know Bob, he is the President of MAFOA and a long time Fiero enthusiast. <P>Personally, I find it sickening that Bob's love for Fiero's is/was or ever has been brought into question.  Anyway, here is the story from Bob's email to the MAFOA egroup list.<P>"I am amazed at the response to MY decision on what to do to MY car.<P>I guess I didn't explain things very well. I have owned this car over 5 years, and have been trying to fix it the entire time. The car seems to have been wrecked, it cannot be fixed to run properly. I have tried numerous<BR>things to work out this problem. It has been to several "experts", who have failed to fix it. The only explanation is that it has been wrecked, and something is bent.<P>I won't go into the other "little" problems I've had with this car. There is not enough time in the day for me to type all of it.<P>This car was no fun to drive, it was only a pain, since it spent so much time in the garage.<P>Now, the only thing left for me to do is to get rid of it. I had several choices. One - to try to pass it off on some sucker that didn't know any better. Two - sell it to someone who thinks they can fix it, and give them all my problems. Three - send it to the crusher. Four - retire the car, and use the good parts to help other Fieros that need a few parts. I chose number four, obviously.<P>So, that is my explanation. Now that the facts are there, if someone still<BR>wants to put me down for my decision, that is their choice. At least they will have the facts, and not be making assumptions."  <P><BR>If Bob says that he has tried everything in his power, he really has.  Looking at it from Bob's point of view, the smart thing would obviously be to part it out to get a FRACTION of the money back that you put into it, and help other Fiero owners in the process.  Now, looking at it through Mr. Save the Fiero's eyes you would dump more and more money into it and never have a decent Fiero come out of it.<P>I guess some people think we should just start a campaign and go to every junk yard in the United States and save all the Fiero's in them, after all in 20 years they will be worth millions since they are so "rare" and such "collector cars" (am I laying it on too thick?).  Fortunately, most of us realize some Fiero's were just not meant for us to enjoy...<P>   <BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||8
Z||000011||FIEROCIOUS||10-22-2000||04:04 PM||||I look at it this way for ever Fiero that is stripped, crushed, trashed, recycled, recked, makes mine increase in value one dollar.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000012||Philphine||10-22-2000||04:40 PM||||they're cars, not shrines. space and cash keeps me from doing it, but i was offered a fiero that supposivly just needs a battery to run. a quick look at one side shows the nose needs replacing an the drivers side door handle is broken. couldn't see the other side. i also know where another is in a junk yard that looks further gone to me but someone else could probaly bring back. for me it would be a matter of getting the nose and a few other parts off the junked car and having one decent one. if you took the preservation reasoning too far you would buy both and more or less have two useless cars, unless like i said before someone comes along who has the skills and/or cash to fix them both. if he dosen't and i decide i don't want to scavenge from one fiero they both go to waste.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.    ||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||10-22-2000||11:02 PM||||As I said in another thread about some stuff going on in CA, You just can't save everything. Doesn't matter how much you want to. You just can't. I try to save what I can from the yards around here but I just don't have the time or the money or the space. Other wise I'd have an inventory pushing kick hill size. I've seen a bunch of Fieros head for the crusher in the last year or so.<P>Basicly anything with major frame damage is crusher food. Get everything off while you can.<P>Everything else is a judgement call. Do you have the time/space to deal with it? can you find another person that does? etc. etc. etc.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fiero5||10-23-2000||12:43 PM||||I figured someone would try and tie this into a recent thread about Bob Schlag's Gold GT. I started this thread partly off of what happened there, as I noticed how many differing opinions there were on the subject. But this has come up many times before, and so i thought it would be interesting to see how everyone felt about it. It was in no means any attempt to thrash on Bob, or anyone else who has ever parted out a car. I was only looking to see what the opinions might be from the many Fiero folks out there on this subject. I opologize if anyone felt this was finger pointing at anyone, as I never meant it to be.<BR> <BR>"This is about Bob Schlag's gold GT he is parting out. For those of you who do not know Bob, he is the President of MAFOA and a long time Fiero enthusiast. Personally, I find it sickening that Bob's love for Fiero's is/was or ever has been brought into question. Anyway."<P>I don't see anything in my begining post on this thread that brings up any of these concerns or mentions Bob Schalg in a bad light, or questions his Fiero loyalty. This is the type of response that get's everyone off track from the original thread. This person is assuming this an attack on someone and it is not.  Let's try and keep focused as all the others have been able to do. <P>"..... Now, looking at it through Mr. Save the Fiero's eyes you would dump more and more money into it and never have a decent Fiero come out of it. I guess some people think we should just start a campaign and go to every junk yard in the United States and save all the Fiero's in them, after all in 20 years they will be worth millions since they are so "rare" and such  "collector cars" (am I laying it on too thick?)." <P>Yes, I would say so. <BR>I ask you to read the begining thread again. Now, let's try and get back to all these differings of opinions. <BR> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Black88GT||10-23-2000||09:21 PM||||Yeah I am sure that your postings to the MAFOA list weren't slamming Bob.  This post on the forum has EVERYTHING to do with Bob, your just trying to get support (other then Brian who everyone now dislikes) for your obvious lost cause.  It seems when everyone got on you for it on the MAFOA egroups list you changed your wording very quickly.  Luckily I was here to post the unbiased facts on the situation. <P>Here is what Ray Paulk had to say about it, I found this interesting...<P>---------------------------------------------<BR>This past Saturday, I was at Bob Parker's Kick Hill Farm picking up the drive train from a parted out Formula. My intent is to use this as a platform to build up a 3.4 / 4-speed auto drive train allowing the three<BR>Fieros we do have to remain in tact until the new set-up is ready.<P>Well, to my surprise, I met Steve Kelley and his Dad there, picking up some Fiero parts.<P>Steve asked me if I had seen the posts to the PRIDE egroup lately. Since I'm way behind on email, I hadn't. Steve kinda filled me in enough to force me to find out more.<P>So... Bo Schlag is parting out his gold Fiero GT. Seems there are a two camps going. 1. Fix up the car or 2. Part it out.<P>Obviously Bob has tried the to fix it up but has given up. I can understand. I have an '87 Bonneville sitting in my yard which is cursed. Rather than dumping more time and $ into it, or passing problems off onto someone else, Bob's decided to part out the car to help others stay on the road. Given the situation, probably the best move.<P>Steve Kelley and Brian Trepkus seem to be the most adamant not to part it out. If you feel this way, try to find a buyer for the entire car, yourself or someone else. Just make sure the new buyer knows the<BR>history.<P>The first car I owned after college was a brand new 1970 Cougar convertible with a 351 Cleveland HP engine. It went like hell (between gas stations). In the owners manual, the minimum recommended octane was<BR>108 (it had a 11.75:1 compression.) At that time we only had leaded gas. Then came the unleaded "crap" with a high octane of 92 or 93. My Cougar would barely run and got only 4 mpg. I decided to sell it. NOW here's<BR>the point..... Several people questioned my sanity in wanting to sell it. "Keep it!" "It will be a prized collectible car in the future!" I replied... "You wanna buy it? For $2,000 you can own and keep it for a<BR>collectible." Funny, no one took me up on this offer (the car had a blue book of like $3k to $4k at the time.)<P>The above anecdote, is similar to Bob's situation. He's got what could be a rare car. But he's had enough of it. He needs to get rid of it but knowing its history, respectfully doesn't want to saddle anyone else with the problems. He's decided to part it out. If you don't like it being parted out, assume the ownership and fix or sell it in tact. Or, in the least, find a buyer for the whole car, one who knows what he/she'd be getting into.<P><B>I must say that I find it ironic that someone who passionately wants to keep as many Fieros on the road as possible was at Kick Hill Farm buying used Fiero parts. Where do these used parts come from? Do you know that most of the cars Bob gets run fine with only minor problems. But these cars get sacrificed so others can stay running. This is the way it is and the way it has to be. We can't rely on enough Fieros getting totaled beyond repair to keep the supply of used parts flowing.</B><P>So if you want to see Bob's gold GT get restored, buy it or find a buyer. Just make sure they know exactly all that Bob has gone through. If you are not willing to do that, then let others know that there are some gold GT parts available. This may help other Fieros keep running longer.<P>Now off my soapbox.<P>Ray Paulk"<BR>---------------------------------------------<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||13
Z||000016||stimpy||10-23-2000||10:01 PM||||I want Gold GT parts...<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Fiero5||10-24-2000||11:22 AM||||Well Jeff, I didn't change my wording. I didn't slam Bob then and I am not slamming him here. This has nothing to do with Bob, except due to your insistance on wanting to continually keep dragging his name in it constantly. Your continued attack of me however, shows who is the one who has the problem here. Get over it allready and let everyone get back to what this post was about, which contrary to your bizaar thinking has nothing to do with Bob Schlag other than similar subject matter. As for Ray Paulk, I posted my reply to his post on the MAFOA list, which is where I believe it should stay.<P>This is a very relevent question and fits in with what this category in Pennocks was meant for. This question has come up many, many times in the past, even before Bob's Gold GT incident. If anyone has an opinion on this subject, when do you think a Fiero should be parted out, feel free to post your feelings.<BR>Thanks.<BR>Sincerely, Steve||||reg||1
Z||000018||mbramble||10-24-2000||11:52 AM||||"If anyone has an opinion on this subject, when do you think a Fiero should be parted out, feel free to post your feelings."<P>Whenever the owner decides he wants to part it out.  We are not talking about national treasures or protected species.  I think it would be quite hypocritical for anyone to say that another person should not part out any car, unless that person was willing to raise the cash to buy the entire car and 'save it' from being parted out. <P>My car would probably have to be in pretty bad shape before I decided to part it out, but that's MY car and I make the decisions about it.  <P>We may all like our little cars - I love mine - but that doesn't gives us the right to tell another what to do with his.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Fiero5||10-24-2000||02:19 PM||||One of the questions that comes up that is really where I was coming from with this post, is not so much the right someone has to do with what they want with their car, as it is your car so you can do what you want. But if you had the ability to make a choice, what would you do and what would be the parameters that would determin your decision one way or the other. Do you feel that the car should be kept as one whenever possible, like major structual damage, or should it not matter.<P>"Here at the Fiero Shop we will part out a car only when there is enough structural damage to make it un-restorable. Until then we try to restore and repair."<P>This is really where I was coming from. Here, the Fiero shop has the ability to make a choice. This is what they try to do whenever possible. If you were in a similar position, what would you do?||||reg||1
Z||000020||mbramble||10-24-2000||05:37 PM||||Not to put too fine of a point on it but maybe the question should be "what is your criteria for determinging the fate (parting out, rebuilding, junking, etc.) of a car you own" making it clear that we are talking about how we look at our own cars, as opposed to how we think others should look at theirs.  <P>Phrases like "When should a Fiero be considered OK to be parted out?", "Should it be OK at anytime...", "Should it be OK only if it has major damage, hit hard...", "... only seriously 'damaged' Fieros be considered used for parting out" make it sound like we're gonna set up a commission to determine when an owner is allowed to part out his car. I know that is not the intent of this post, but considering other recent mail list posts I have seen on the subject, it's not hard to make that leap.<P>To answer that question, if something happened to my car (accident, rust, vandalism, major failure, etc.) I would determine if it would be economically feasible to put the car back into the shape where I could enjoy it again.  If not, I would use it for parts -- either for another car I'd buy, or to sell to other enthusiasts to finance the puchase of another car that didn't need my spare parts. ||||reg||1
